===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# Thin [[typing]] [[paper]] used to make multiple copies.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1977|author=w:John Le Carré|title=The Honourable Schoolboy|page=251|publisher=Folio Society|year_published=2010
|passage=Smiley peered once more at the '''flimsy''' which he still clutched in his pudgy hand.}}
# {{lb|en|naval|_|slang}} A [[service]] [[certificate]]
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1964|author=Australia. Parliament|title=Records of the Proceedings and Printed Papers of the Parliament
|passage=A perusal of the comments of officers under whom he has served as recorded in his “'''flimsies'''" indicates that he has almost consistently received high commendation for his service.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1994|author=John Wells|title=The Royal Navy: An Illustrated Social History, 1870-1982|page=7
|passage=Regulations required a commanding officer to render annual confidential reports on the character and ability of his officers - with particular reference to sobriety - on forms known as ''''flimsies''''.}}
# {{lb|en|informal|in the plural}} Skimpy [[underwear]].
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=October 25, 2007|author=Ruth La Ferla|title=Now It’s Nobody’s Secret|work=New York Times|url=http://www.nytimes.com/2007/10/25/fashion/shows/25LINGERIE.html
|passage=Choosing lingerie “is about what makes you look good, but also what looks good with or through your clothing,” said Monica Mitro, a spokeswoman for Victoria’s Secret, the brand that catapulted racy '''flimsies''' into the public eye.}}
# {{lb|en|slang}} A [[banknote]].
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1846|author=George William MacArthur Reynolds|title=The Mysteries of London|page=60
|passage={{...}} Q was a Queer-screen, that served as a blind; / R was a Reader, with '''flimsies''' well lined; {{...}} }}

